  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nRain to Snow Today through Monday...A couple of narrow strips of \nlight rain developed ahead of the approaching cold front early this \nafternoon extending from central to far northeast WI. Meanwhile, the \naforementioned cold front was entering northwest WI. Rain is \nexpected to increase in coverage with the arrival of the cold front \nthis evening into tonight. The rain will finally reach east-central \nWI throughout Sunday morning as the front gains some speed. The \nhighest rainfall amounts will stay focused mainly northwest of a \nMarshfield to Antigo to Florence line where probabilities for rain \nover 1 inch are greatest (60-90%). Locations southeast of this line \nare more likely to see values of 0.25-0.50\" due to the front gaining \nspeed into early Sunday. The rain will come to an end by Sunday \nafternoon with the departure of the front. In previous forecasts, \nthere was a signal for the rain transitioning to snow on the back \nside of the front as colder air filters into the area with a \nnorthwest flow, especially across north-central WI; however, the \nsignal has decreased. This appears to be related to there being too \nmuch of a low-level westerly wind component compared to the more \nfavorable northwesterly wind component. So, while there is still \na very low chance for lake-effect snow showers/flurries Sunday \nafternoon/evening along the WI/Upper MI border, the better signal \narrives Sunday night into Monday for the Northwoods lake-effect \nsnow belt regions, including Door County, when winds turn more \nnorthwesterly. By Monday evening, the probability for snowfall \namounts of 0.5\" or more have slightly expanded to the far north- \ncentral WI lake-effect snow belt region where probs are 15-60%; \nthe highest probs (40-60%) are still focused across Vilas County. \nThere are also signs of a more focused lake-effect snow band \ndeveloping, which could lead to slightly higher snowfall amounts. \nAs a final note, winds accompanying the cold front, ahead of and \nbehind, will make for a fairly breezy period this afternoon \nthrough Monday, with gusts up to 25 mph at times.\n\nFollowing this system, dry conditions are expected to return and \nlast through Wednesday. Models are indicating southern stream energy \nlifting into the Great Lakes region on Thanksgiving Day, which may \nbring a mix of rain and snow to portions of the region. However, \nthere is a large deal of uncertainty in the evolution of the upper-\nlevel pattern during this time. Stay tuned!\n\nTemperatures...Southerly flow continues to aid in the prevailing \nwarmer air this afternoon, resulting in temperatures soaring into \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s today, or about 20-25 degrees above normal. \nThese well above normal temperatures will come to an end with the \npassage of the cold front tonight. However, temperatures on Sunday \nwill still be about 5 degrees above normal with values ranging from \nthe low 40s to mid 50s. Temperatures will then fall to near or \nslightly below normal for the start of next work week with highs in \nthe 30s and 40s.",
